  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe propose an iterative convex optimization approach using CVXPY to find step function h that minimizes the maximum overlap integral. The problem's non-convexity due to h_i h_j terms is addressed through disciplined convex programming. The solver efficiently handles constraints while maintaining feasibility.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nWe model the problem as:\n- Variables: h \u2208 [0,1]^n, C \u2208 \u211d\n- Objective: minimize C\n- Constraints:\n  1. \u2211h_i = n/2\n  2. For each shift k \u2260 0, \u2211h_i(1 - h_{i+k}) \u2264 C\nThis formulation leverages CVXPY's ability to handle quadratic constraints.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Parent validation:\n   - Use initial_h_values if available\n   - Normalize to sum(h) = n/2 and clip to [0,1]\n\nb. CVXPY setup:\n   - Define variables h and C\n   - Formulate constraints for all shifts\n   - Solve with ECOS solver\n\nc. Post-solve projection:\n   - Clip and rescale h to ensure feasibility\n\n4. Search choices\n- Use ECOS solver with default settings\n- Maximum iterations: 100\n- Feasibility tolerance: 1e-5\n\n5. Compute budget\nEstimate 600s runtime with CVXPY solving in ~100s\nRobustness through feasibility checks and projection\n\n6. Validation\nEnsure h meets constraints and compute actual C\u2085",
